<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.14.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01998</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.14.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>f8fea744-60c8-4aa7-8588-83b2762eb23f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise data-content-id="af85d497-4717-4fc4-b6c7-2aacde1d05e9" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Each represents the height of an object being launched into the air as a function of time.</para>
            <para>Here are graphs of functions \(f\) and \(g\).</para>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="Graphs of functions f and g. Vertical axis, height. Horizontal axis, time. Function f begins low on the y axis, increases in height quickly, then ends at about halfway down the x axis. Function g begins high on the y axis, moves gradually downward, and ends toward the end of the x axis.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_08_16_11_01.svg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para> Which object was launched from a higher point?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! The object represented by function \(g\) because its \(y\)-intercepts is above the \(y\)-intercepts of the graph of \(f\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: The object represented by function \(g\) because its \(y\)-intercept is above the \(y\)-intercept of the graph of \(f\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="4beb467c-0d2f-4c24-8881-b4da9aa1f763" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Which object reached a higher point?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The object represented by function \(f\) because the vertex is higher.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: The object represented by function \(f\) because the vertex is higher. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="c35c6425-b4dc-4a7b-aad6-ad79eadee4c0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Which object was launched with the greater upward velocity?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The object represented by function \(f\) because the “slope” near the \(y\)-intercept is greater. It increases in height much more quickly than the other object.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3. </para>
            <para> The correct answer is: The object represented by function \(f\) because the “slope” near the \(y\)-intercept is greater. It increases in height much more quickly than the other object. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="a6dc30bc-7294-4722-b69f-d4256a3b6afc" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Which object landed last?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The object represented by function \(g\) because the positive \(x\)-intercept of its graph is to the right of the positive \(x\)-intercept of the graph of \(f\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: The object represented by function \(g\) because the positive \(x\)-intercept of its graph is to the right of the positive \(x\)-intercept of the graph of \(f\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="1d3584d2-ddc0-45ef-9f9f-eeacd9c57fcf" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>The function \(h\) given by \(h(t)=(1−t)(8+16t)\) models the height of a ball in feet, \(t\) seconds after it was thrown.</para>
            <para>Find one of the zeros of the functions using \((8+16t)\). </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! -0.5. From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at \(t=-0.5\). The zero of the function is the same value. </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is -0.5. From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at \(t=-0.5\). The zero of the function is the same value. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="fd7745a3-fd5a-4fb2-a162-97e44cdd044d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>Find one of the zeros of the functions using \((1-t)\). </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! 1. From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at \((1-t)\). The zero of the function is the same value. </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 1. From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at \((1-t)\). The zero of the function is the same value. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="e1af55f5-cc64-4b14-9a0e-6834b1cdaa8b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> What do the zeros tell us in this situation?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>The positive zero means the time, in seconds, when the basketball is on the ground or hits the ground.</para>
            <para>The negative zero represents the height, in feet, when the time is zero.</para>
            <para>The positive zero represents the time, in seconds, when the basketball reaches the maximum height.</para>
            <para>The negative zero represents the time, in seconds, when the basketball is on the ground or hits the ground.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The positive zero means the time, in seconds, when the basketball is on the ground or hits the ground.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is: The positive zero means the time, in seconds, when the basketball is on the ground or hits the ground. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="a699ac98-cd91-467f-9025-d5fef8e137fd" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Are both zeros meaningful?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! No. Only the positive zero is meaningful. It tells us the ball hits the ground after 1 second. </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para>Incorrect. The correct answer is No. Only the positive zero is meaningful. It tells us the ball hits the ground after 1 second. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="705d5199-4f67-4611-ae30-30ff51aa5a85" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>From what height, in feet, is the ball thrown? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! 8 feet. When \((1−t)(8+16t)\) is rewritten in standard form it is \(8+8t−16t^2\). The constant term (which corresponds to the \(y\)-intercept) tells us the height of the basketball when \(t=0\) or when it is launched. </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is 8 feet. When \((1−t)(8+16t)\) is rewritten in standard form it is \(8+8t−16t^2\). The constant term (which corresponds to the \(y\)-intercept) tells us the height of the basketball when \(t=0\) or when it is launched. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="29628f71-310e-42f0-886f-689c67ea88a4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>When, in seconds, does the ball reach its highest point? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! 0.25 seconds. Graphing \(h\) shows the maximum point at (0.25,9). Or, you could recognize that 0.25 is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex because it is halfway between the two zeros, -0.5 and 1.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 0.25 seconds. Graphing \(h\) shows the maximum point at (0.25,9). Or, you could recognize that 0.25 is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex because it is halfway between the two zeros, -0.5 and 1. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="5ab1f1c1-e6d5-43fa-9777-85412039fd00" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>How high, in feet, does the ball go? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 9 feet. Graphing \(h\) shows the maximum point at (0.25,9). Or, you could recognize that 0.25 is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex because it is halfway between the two zeros, -0.5 and 1. Then evaluate the function at 0.25 to get a height of 9.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.14.3 – 7.14.5.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 9 feet. Graphing \(h\) shows the maximum point at (0.25,9). Or, you could recognize that 0.25 is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex because it is halfway between the two zeros, -0.5 and 1. Then evaluate the function at 0.25 to get a height of 9. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="e3be73f2-3cb1-46b0-9b1d-e06668db6158" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The height in feet of a thrown football is modeled by the equation \(f(t)=6+30t−16t^2\), where time \(t\) is measured in seconds.</para>
            <para> Which term in the equation describes how many feet above the ground the football was thrown?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>The constant term 6</para>
            <para>The linear term \(30t\)</para>
            <para>The squared term \(-16t^2\)</para>
            <para>The squared term \(16t^2\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The football was thrown from 6 feet above the ground.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is the constant term 6. The football was thrown from 6 feet above the ground. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="4db11e62-1a36-48c7-8734-ce008601fe62" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Which term in the equation describes the initial upward velocity of the football in feet per second?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>The constant term 6</para>
            <para>The linear term \(30t\)</para>
            <para>The squared term \(-16t^2\)</para>
            <para>The squared term \(16t^2\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The initial upward velocity of the football was 30 feet per second. </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is the linear term \(30t\). The initial upward velocity of the football was 30 feet per second. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="ca5820b3-7859-4636-b616-c1c22fe6dcfe" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> How does the squared term \(-16t^2\) affect the value of the function \(f\)? </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>The squared term decreases the value of the function.</para>
            <para>The squared term increases the value of the function.</para>
            <para>The squared term has no effect on the value of the function.</para>
            <para>The value of the function is zero when \(−16t^2\) is zero.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The squared term decreases the value of the function because the values of \(16t^2\) are being subtracted from \(6+30t\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: The squared term decreases the value of the function because the values of \(16t^2\) are being subtracted from \(6+30t\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="5d14125b-5d97-4607-9fa6-d7202ce485dc" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> What does this term \(−16t^2\) reveal about the situation?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>The influence of gravity pulling the ball down to the ground.</para>
            <para>The initial upward velocity of the football.</para>
            <para>How far the football was thrown above the ground.</para>
            <para>How long the ball is in the air before it hits the ground.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The influence of gravity pulling the ball down to the ground.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: The influence of gravity pulling the ball down to the ground. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="321a83e9-c3ef-4fff-a1bc-23a581947f8e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>The height in feet of an arrow is modeled by the equation \(h(t)=(1+2t)(18−8t)\), where \(t\) is seconds after the arrow is shot.</para>
            <para> At what time, in seconds, does the arrow hit the ground? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! The positive zero is 2.25 because \((18−8t)\) is equal to zero when \(t=2.25\). The zeros mean the times, in seconds, when the arrow is on the ground. Only the positive zero makes sense. The negative zero would represent a time before the arrow was launched.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 2.25. The positive zero is 2.25 because \((18−8t)\) is equal to zero when \(t=2.25\). The zeros mean the times, in seconds, when the arrow is on the ground. Only the positive zero makes sense. The negative zero would represent a time before the arrow was launched. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="96622d8a-7db3-4b49-921b-b8debb4eee4a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
            <para>At what height, in feet, is the arrow shot? </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 18 feet. When the arrow is shot, \(t\) is 0 and h(0)=(1+2⋅0)(18−8⋅0)=(1)(18)=18.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.5.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 18 feet. When the arrow is shot, \(t\) is 0 and \(h(0)=(1+2⋅0)(18−8⋅0)=(1)(18)=18\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="a9419388-3cfa-45be-9186-903475b2c12e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>5. Two objects are launched into the air.</para>
            <para>The height, in feet, of Object A is given by the equation \(f(t)=4+32t−16t^2\).</para>
            <para>The height, in feet, of Object B is given by the equation \(g(t)=2.5+40t−16^2\).</para>
            <para>In both functions, \(t\) is seconds after launch.</para>
            <para> Which object was launched from a greater height?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Object A. The objects are launched when \(t=0\). \(f(0)=4+32⋅0−16(0)2=4\) and \(g(0)=2.5+40⋅0−16(0)2=2.5\).</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: Object A. The objects are launched when \(t=0\). \(f(0)=4+32⋅0−16(0)2=4\) and \(g(0)=2.5+40⋅0−16(0)2=2.5\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise data-content-id="214ffa65-1827-44d9-bc43-c1b25a0f12fa" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> Which object was launched with a greater upward velocity?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! Object B.The coefficient of \(t\) gives the upward velocity and 40 is greater than 32.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.14.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: Object B. The coefficient of \(t\) gives the upward velocity and 40 is greater than 32. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
